## <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # What's in Your Bag? – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Discovering Our Classroom</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> What's in Your Bag?</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Compose short texts using simple phrases and sentences. Interactive: Respond to written questions from peers.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can compose short texts about classroom items using simple phrases and sentences (e.g., "This is a pencil."). Can respond to written questions from peers about classroom topics (e.g., "Where is the book?" "It is near the teacher."). Can write a short note to a peer using familiar language (e.g., "Hello, Elena! Your bag is on the floor.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying a large, colorful image of a school bag with various classroom items (e.g., a book, a pencil, a ruler, a notebook, a crayon, an eraser) visible inside and around it. Students orally identify the items. The teacher then asks students to briefly write down 2-3 items they see on a small piece of paper or a mini-whiteboard, initiating the writing skill.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing simple sentences about the items from the image on the board, emphasizing correct spelling and simple sentence structure. For example, the teacher writes: "This is a book. My pencil is here. It is a blue crayon." The teacher clearly pronounces target sounds like /b/ in "book" and /t/ in "table" (if pointing to a table next to the bag) and blends phonemes for words like "on" /ɔn/ and "paper" /ˈp-eɪp-ər/ as they write or speak.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the written sentences and vocabulary.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this a book or a marker?" (Pointing to the written word "book") - Expected answer: "A book."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you write 'ruler' on your paper?" (Teacher observes students' attempts.)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short, simple written "Classroom Description" text with accompanying pictures of the classroom layout. The text uses target vocabulary (desk, book, marker, board, shelf, eraser, paper, chair, crayon, notebook, ruler, pencil, pencil case, pen, map) and grammar (WH-questions, prepositions of place, Present simple).
                <br>Example Text: "Hello, students! Look at our classroom. The board is large. My desk is neat. There is a green chair next to my desk. My pencil case is on the desk. What's in my pencil case? I have a pen, a pencil, and a colorful eraser. The book is on the shelf. The map is on the board."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understanding written descriptions of objects and their locations within a familiar classroom context, using appropriate prepositions (e.g., "The book is on the table.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the text silently and then:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is this text about?" (Students write their answer in one or two words, e.g., "Our classroom" or "Classroom things").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read sentences on the board and write "T" or "F".
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The board is small."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"My pencil case is on the desk."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The book is on the chair."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing simple sentences, WH-questions ("What's in your bag?", "Where is the book?"), and using prepositions of place (in, on, next to, between, behind) with classroom vocabulary. They develop phonemic awareness by blending /ɔ/ /-n/ in "on" as they write.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Fill in the Blanks & Labeling:</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with pictures of classroom items in specific locations. Students complete sentences with missing vocabulary words or prepositions. For example:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The (pencil) is (on) the (desk)." (Image: a pencil on a desk)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The (book) is (in) the (bag)." (Image: a book inside a bag)</li>
                        </ul>
                        Students also label a simple diagram of a classroom with words like 'board', 'desk', 'chair', 'shelf'.
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Sentence Building:</b> The teacher provides word prompts (e.g., "ruler / next to / notebook" or "eraser / in / pencil case"). Students write complete sentences using these words.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "The ruler is next to the notebook."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "The eraser is in the pencil case."</li>
                        </ul>
                        The teacher encourages students to pay attention to the initial sounds /b/ in "book" and /t/ in "table" as they write or silently read their sentences.
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Practice – Descriptive Sentences & Peer Check:</b> In pairs, students describe a picture of a "messy desk" or "organized shelf" provided by the teacher. Each student writes 2-3 sentences describing where items are located, using possessive adjectives (my, his, her, your) and adverbs (here, there). For example: "The book is on the desk. My pen is next to the ruler." Partners then exchange their written descriptions and check for correct vocabulary, prepositions, and sentence structure, respectfully asking "Is the book on the desk or under the desk?" to confirm.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Written Product (Descriptive paragraph and a short note).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. "My Desk/Bag Description":</b> Students draw their own desk or the inside of their school bag, depicting 3-4 classroom items (e.g., book, pencil, ruler, notebook, crayon). Below their drawing, they write a short descriptive paragraph (3-4 simple sentences) using the target vocabulary (e.g., pen, pencil case, paper), prepositions of place (in, on, next to), and the Present Simple. Example: "This is my desk. My book is on the desk. My pencil case is next to the book. My red crayon is in my pencil case."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. "A Note to a Friend":</b> Students imagine a peer has lost an item. They choose one classroom item (e.g., a ruler, an eraser) and write a short, simple note to a classmate describing where they "found" or "placed" it. They use familiar language and appropriate prepositions. Example: "Hello, Elena! Your ruler is here. It is on the shelf, next to the blue marker. See you!" (This addresses the "respond to written questions from peers" and "write a short note" learning outcomes, promoting respectful classroom interactions).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment and reflection)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> The teacher collects the "My Desk/Bag Description" and "A Note to a Friend" written products. A simple checklist or rubric is used to assess student writing based on the evaluation criteria.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' written descriptions and notes are reviewed by the teacher.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Used at least 3 target vocabulary words (e.g., book, pencil, desk, ruler, marker).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Used at least 2 prepositions of place correctly (e.g., in, on, next to, behind).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Composed 2-3 complete, simple sentences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Message in the note is clear and understandable.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write sentences about things in my bag/on my desk." (Circle: Yes / Almost / Not yet)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can use 'in' and 'on' correctly in my writing." (Circle: Yes / Almost / Not yet)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">4-6 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Consolidation and connection to next lesson)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you write today that describe your classroom or bag?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult about writing a note to your friend?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students compose short texts about classroom items and write notes?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific adjustments or additional scaffolds (e.g., more sentence starters, vocabulary review) are needed for students who struggled with sentence structure or preposition usage in their writing?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "In our next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration), we will use our written notes and descriptions to play a classroom scavenger hunt game, verbally confirming object locations, and consolidating everything we've learned about 'What's in Your Bag?' and 'Discovering Our Classroom'."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">4-5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>